n a story; greeting chart: language diversity; respect plan: living together fairly. uses sources and places appropriately for this lesson.","options":["Copy the worked example and call it independent work.","Culture web: parts of culture; origin map: places in a story; greeting chart: language diversity; respect plan: living together fairly.","A display of flags and food pictures with no sources or permission shows stereotypes rather than understanding.","Ignore the task and invent an unrelated answer."],"question":"Which response best shows careful thinking about review?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows.","options":["Make a bigger claim without naming any source or cue.","Copy a classmate’s sentence with no local detail.","A display of flags and food pictures with no sources or permission shows stereotypes rather than understanding.","Name a specific source, cue or example and say what it shows about review."],"question":"Which habit strengthens evidence about culture and explain why it matters?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea with concrete detail.","options":["Treat a missing detail as a proven fact.","Ignore the task and invent an unrelated answer.","Example showcase: a display with a culture web, a family origin map, a migration story, a language greeting chart and a respect plan, each…","A display of flags and food pictures with no sources or permission shows stereotypes rather than understanding."],"question":"Using this lesson idea — The cultures and communities review links culture, family origins, migration stories, interviewing with… — which choice is best?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Respect and accuracy mean checking evidence and honouring limits/protocols.","options":["Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster.","Treat every online claim as equally reliable.","Check facts/cues, acknowledge limits, and avoid inventing details.","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete."],"question":"Which action shows respect and accuracy together?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the skill.","options":["Copy the worked example and call it independent work.","A display of flags and food pictures with no sources or permission shows stereotypes rather than understanding.","Assemble a cultures and communities showcase using the checklist. Check that you have permission for every personal item. Write a one-minute talk…","Retype the worked example with no new example."],"question":"Which task best shows independent use of review?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating review on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: Culture web: parts of culture; origin map: places in a story; greeting chart: language diversity; respect plan: living together fairly. Reasoning: I used review by attending to culture and explain why it matters rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason.","question":"Name one likely error with review, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Common error: A display of flags and food pictures with no sources or permission shows stereotypes rather than understanding. Correction: Culture web: parts of culture; origin map: places in a story; greeting chart: language diversity; respect plan: living together fairly. Why: the correction restores accurate use of review.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-hsie-y3-w35-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA HSIE alignment using catalogue outcome codes (HS2-*); content-point mapping and local First Nations protocols remain pending human review.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise review.","Read the mission before you start mapping or using sources.","Keep the place, people or source you are studying in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your evidence with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the source or place detail that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore sources or respectful practice.","Prefer accurate evidence over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your evidence and reason are both present.","Proofread names, places, dates and sequence.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact source detail or place feature before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key evidence.","Say what each source or place detail shows about the idea.","A final label alone is weaker than recorded evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak claim with stronger evidence.","Change the claim or use of sources, not only how neat the poster looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh place, person or source that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original evidence and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new sources or places."]},"subject":"History","success_criteria":["I can use review with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify culture and explain why it matters.","I can practise respect independently on a new prompt.","I can notice showcase needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"The cultures and communities review links culture, family origins, migration stories, interviewing with care, traditions and festivals, sharing, languages, welcoming newcomers and living together respectfully. Supported records follow the actual sources and purpose of the task.…","options":["A claim unrelated to the source or place.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no evidence.","The cultures and communities review links culture, family origins, migration stories, interviewing with care, traditions and festivals, sharing, languages, welcoming newcomers and living together respectfully."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about review?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample showcase: a display with a culture web, a family origin map, a migration story, a language greeting chart and a respect plan, each with sources named and permission noted.\n\nTRY FIRST\nMatch each product to what it shows: culture web; origin map; greeting chart; respect plan.\n\nFEEDBACK\nCulture web: parts of culture; origin map: places in a story; greeting chart: language diversity; respect plan: living together fairly.","explain":"The cultures and communities review links culture, family origins, migration stories, interviewing with care, traditions and festivals, sharing, languages, welcoming newcomers and living together respectfully.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"A display of flags and food pictures with no sources or permission shows stereotypes rather than understanding. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Evidence from the unit.","caption":"Permission for personal items.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Web</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">culture parts</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Map</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">family places</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which source or place detail matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: Example showcase: a display with a culture web, a family origin map, a migration story, a language greeting chart and a  Then write your own evidence.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which source or place detail matters most.\nJuno: Name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: Example showcase: a display with a culture web, a family origin map, a migration story, a language greeting chart and a  Then write your own evidence."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Make the HSIE idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim. Supported records follow the actual sources and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence.","options":["Make the HSIE idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim.","A claim unrelated to the source or place.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no evidence."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about review?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample showcase: a display with a culture web, a family origin map, a migration story, a language greeting chart and a respect plan, each with sources named and permission noted. Look at each observation, source detail or example that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key observations, sources or steps from the model and say what each one shows.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful record names the evidence and the reason it supports the claim, not only a label.","explain":"Make the HSIE idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim 